==Trivia==
*The watermelon-punching [[Titan Cat (Normal Cat)|Titan]] icon might be a reference to ''[[wikipedia:Suikawari|Suikawari]]'', a traditional summertime game which involves splitting a watermelon with a stick while blindfolded.
*The red and white [[Kotatsu Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Octopus Cat]] may be a reference to ''[[wikipedia:List of Game & Watch games#Octopus|Game & Watch Octopus]]'', as some editions of the game have a red and white outer shell.
*Several of the icons from the [[Anniversary/11th Year|11th Anniversary]] Wildcat Slots appear to make references in line with the event's fantasy RPG theme:
**The [[Media:Wildcat slots pogo cat.png|icon]] of [[Pogo Cat (Rare Cat)|Pogo Cat]] on a sword might have been inspired by Link's [[wikia:zelda:Down Thrust|Down Thrust]] from the ''[[wikipedia:The Legend of Zelda|The Legend of Zelda]]'' series.
**The [[Media:Wildcat slots madam bride.png|icon]] of [[Madam Bride (Rare Cat)|Madam Bride]] dressed as a princess has a similar color scheme to [https://dragon-quest.org/wiki/Princess_Gwaelin Princess Gwaelin] from the ''[[wikipedia:Dragon Quest|Dragon Quest]]'' series.
**The [[Media:Wildcat slots mimikotatsu.png|icon]] of [[Kotatsu Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Kotatsu Cat]] being eaten by a treasure chest with teeth is based on the [[wikipedia:Mimic (Dungeons & Dragons)|Mimic]], a treasure chest-mimicking monster from ''[[wikipedia:Dungeons & Dragons|Dungeons & Dragons]]''.
**The [[Media:Wildcat slots titan tsubo.png|icon]] of [[Titan Cat (Normal Cat)|Titan Cat]] punching a [[Jar Base (Enemy Base)|Jar Base]], like the Jar Base itself, may be a reference to the pots from ''The Legend of Zelda''.
**The [[Media:Slot 004.png|art assets]] for the 11th anniversary Wildcat Slots include a cat shaped just like a [https://dragon-quest.org/wiki/Slime Slime] from ''Dragon Quest'', next to which is the [[wikipedia:Triforce|Triforce]] from ''The Legend of Zelda''.

